L11: # Tracing
L12: 
L13: Spring Boot Actuator provides dependency management and auto-configuration for 【6†Micrometer Tracing†docs.micrometer.io】, a facade for popular tracer libraries.
L14: 
L15:  | To learn more about Micrometer Tracing capabilities, see its 【6†reference documentation†docs.micrometer.io】.
L16: 
L17: ## Supported Tracers
L18: 
L19: Spring Boot ships auto-configuration for the following tracers:
L20: 
L21:   * 【7†OpenTelemetry†opentelemetry.io】 with 【8†OTLP†opentelemetry.io】.
L22: 
L23:   * 【9†OpenZipkin Brave†github.com】 with 【10†Zipkin†zipkin.io】.
L24: ## Getting Started
L25: 
L26: We need an example application that we can use to get started with tracing. For our purposes, the simple “Hello World!” web application that’s covered in the 【11†Developing Your First Spring Boot Application】 section will suffice. We’re going to use the Brave tracer with Zipkin as trace backend.
L27: 
L28: To recap, our main application code looks like this:
L29: 
L30:   * Java
L31:   * Kotlin
L32: 
L33:     `import org.apache.commons.logging.Log;
L34:     import org.apache.commons.logging.LogFactory;
L35: 
L36:     import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
L37:     import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
L38:     import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestMapping;
L39:     import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
L40: 
L41:     @RestController
L42:     @SpringBootApplication
L43:     public class MyApplication {
L44:     	private static final Log logger = LogFactory.getLog(MyApplication.class);
L45: 
L46:     	@RequestMapping("/")
L47:     	String home() {
L48:     		logger.info("home() has been called");
L49:     		return "Hello World!";
L50:     	}
L51: 
L52:     	public static void main(String[] args) {
L53:     		SpringApplication.run(MyApplication.class, args);
L54:     	}
L55: 
L56:     }`
