L82: 
L83: Now we have to add the `org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-zipkin` dependency.
L84: 
L85: Then add the following application properties:
L86: 
L87:   * Properties
L88: 
L89:   * YAML
L90: 
L91:     `management.tracing.sampling.probability=1`
L92: 
L93:     `management:
L94:       tracing:
L95:         sampling:
L96:           probability: 1.0`
L97: By default, Spring Boot samples only 10% of requests to prevent overwhelming the trace backend. This property switches it to 100% so that every request is sent to the trace backend.
L98: 
L99: To collect and visualize the traces, we need a running trace backend. We use Zipkin as our trace backend here. The 【12†Zipkin Quickstart guide†zipkin.io】 provides instructions how to start Zipkin locally.
L100: 
L101: After Zipkin is running, you can start your application.
L102: If you open a web browser to `【13†localhost:8080†localhost】`, you should see the following output:
L103: 
L104:     `Hello World!`
L105: 
L106: Behind the scenes, an observation has been created for the HTTP request, which in turn gets bridged to Brave, which reports a new trace to Zipkin.
L107: 
L108: Now open the Zipkin UI at `【14†localhost:9411†localhost】` and press the "Run Query" button to list all collected traces. You should see one trace. Press the "Show" button to see the details of that trace.
L109: ## Logging Correlation IDs
L110: 
L111: Correlation IDs provide a helpful way to link lines in your log files to spans/traces. If you are using Micrometer Tracing, Spring Boot will include correlation IDs in your logs by default.
L112: The default correlation ID is built from `traceId` and `spanId` 【15†MDC†logback.qos.ch】 values. For example, if Micrometer Tracing has added an MDC `traceId` of `803B448A0489F84084905D3093480352` and an MDC `spanId` of `3425F23BB2432450` the log output will include the correlation ID `[803B448A0489F84084905D3093480352-3425F23BB2432450]`.
L113: If you prefer to use a different format for your correlation ID, you can use the `logging.pattern.correlation` property to define one. For example, the following will provide a correlation ID for Logback in format previously used by Spring Cloud Sleuth:
L114: 
L115:   * Properties
L116:   * YAML
L117: 
L118:     `logging.pattern.correlation=[${spring.application.name:},%X{traceId:-},%X{spanId:-}]
L119:     logging.include-application-name=false`
L120: 
L121:     `logging:
L122:       pattern:
L123:         correlation: "[${spring.application.name:},%X{traceId:-},%X{spanId:-}] "
L124:       include-application-name: false`
L125:  | In the example above, `logging.include-application-name` is set to `false` to avoid the application name being duplicated in the log messages (`logging.pattern.correlation` already contains it). It’s also worth mentioning that `logging.pattern.correlation` contains a trailing space so that it is separated from the logger name that comes right after it by default.
L126: 
L127:  | Correlation IDs rely on context propagation. Please read 【16†this documentation for more details】.
L128: ## Propagating Traces
L129: 
L130: To automatically propagate traces over the network, use the auto-configured 【17†`RestTemplateBuilder`】, 【18†`RestClient.Builder`】 or 【19†`WebClient.Builder`】 to construct the client.
L131: 
L132:  | If you create the 【20†`RestTemplate`】, the 【21†`RestClient`】 or the 【22†`WebClient`】 without using the auto-configured builders, automatic trace propagation won’t work!
L133: ## Tracer Implementations
L134: 
L135: As Micrometer Tracer supports multiple tracer implementations, there are multiple dependency combinations possible with Spring Boot. The combinations OpenTelemetry with OTLP and Brave with Zipkin are common and have dedicated starters.
L136: ### OpenTelemetry With OTLP
L137: 
L138: Tracing with OpenTelemetry and reporting using OTLP requires the following dependencies:
L139: 
L140:   * `org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-opentelemetry`
L141: 
L142: Use the `management.opentelemetry.tracing.export.otlp.*` configuration properties to configure reporting using OTLP.
L143:  | If you need to apply advanced customizations to OTLP span exporters, consider registering 【23†`OtlpHttpSpanExporterBuilderCustomizer`】 or 【24†`OtlpGrpcSpanExporterBuilderCustomizer`】 beans. These will be invoked before the creation of the `OtlpHttpSpanExporter` or `OtlpGrpcSpanExporter`. The customizers take precedence over anything applied by the auto-configuration.
L144: ### OpenTelemetry With Zipkin
L145: 
L146:  | OpenTelemetry has 【25†deprecated their Zipkin support†opentelemetry.io】. The auto-configuration for it will be removed in Spring Boot 4.2. Either switch to Brave or consider using 【26†the Zipkin OTel module†github.com】 for ingesting OTLP directly.
L147: 
L148: Tracing with OpenTelemetry and reporting to Zipkin requires the following dependencies:
L149:   * `org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-micrometer-tracing-opentelemetry` - Spring Boot’s support for Micrometer Tracing over OpenTelemetry.
L150: 
L151:   * `io.micrometer:micrometer-tracing-bridge-otel` - bridges the Micrometer Observation API to OpenTelemetry.
L152: 
L153:   * `org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-zipkin` - Spring Boot’s support for Zipkin.
L154: 
L155:   * `io.opentelemetry:opentelemetry-exporter-zipkin` - OpenTelemetry exporter that reports traces to Zipkin.
L156: Use the `management.tracing.export.zipkin.*` configuration properties to configure reporting to Zipkin.
L157: ### OpenZipkin Brave With Zipkin
L158: 
L159: Tracing with OpenZipkin Brave and reporting to Zipkin requires the following dependencies:
L160: 
L161:   * `org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-zipkin`
L162: 
L163: Use the `management.tracing.export.zipkin.*` configuration properties to configure reporting to Zipkin.
L164: ## Sampling
L165: 
L166: By default, Spring Boot samples only 10% of requests to prevent overwhelming the trace backend. The `management.tracing.sampling.probability` property can be used to configure this.
L167: 
L168: When using OpenTelemetry, you can also configure which sampler is used via the `management.opentelemetry.tracing.sampler` property. The following samplers are supported:
L169: Sampler  | Description
L170: --- | ---
L171: `always-on`  | Samples every trace.
L172: `always-off`  | Discards every trace.
L173: `trace-id-ratio`  | Samples a fraction of traces based on `management.tracing.sampling.probability`.
L174: `parent-based-always-on`  | If the parent span is sampled, samples the child span. If there is no parent, samples every trace.
L175: `parent-based-always-off`  | If the parent span is sampled, samples the child span. If there is no parent, discards every trace.
