L63: ### WebClient Runtime
L64: 
L65: Spring Boot will auto-detect which 【13†`ClientHttpConnector`】 to use to drive 【6†`WebClient`】 depending on the libraries available on the application classpath. In order of preference, the following clients are supported:
L66: 
L67:   1. Reactor Netty
L68: 
L69:   2. Jetty RS client
L70: 
L71:   3. Apache HttpClient
L72: 
L73:   4. JDK HttpClient
L74: 
L75: If multiple clients are available on the classpath, the most preferred client will be used.
L76: The `spring-boot-starter-webflux` starter depends on `io.projectreactor.netty:reactor-netty` by default, which brings both server and client implementations. If you choose to use Jetty as a reactive server instead, you should add a dependency on the Jetty Reactive HTTP client library, `org.eclipse.jetty:jetty-reactive-httpclient`. Using the same technology for server and client has its advantages, as it will automatically share HTTP resources between client and server.
L77: Developers can override the resource configuration for Jetty and Reactor Netty by providing a custom 【14†`ReactorResourceFactory`】 or 【15†`JettyResourceFactory`】 bean - this will be applied to both clients and servers.
L78: 
L79: If you wish to override that choice for the client, you can define your own 【13†`ClientHttpConnector`】 bean and have full control over the client configuration.
L80: 
L81: You can learn more about the 【16†`WebClient` configuration options in the Spring Framework reference documentation】.
L82: ### Global HTTP Connector Configuration
L83: 
L84: If the auto-detected 【13†`ClientHttpConnector`】 does not meet your needs, you can use the `spring.http.clients.reactive.connector` property to pick a specific connector. For example, if you have Reactor Netty on your classpath, but you prefer Jetty’s 【17†`HttpClient`†javadoc.jetty.org】 you can add the following:
L85: 
L86:   * Properties
L87:   * YAML
L88: 
L89:     `spring.http.clients.reactive.connector=jetty`
L90: 
L91:     `spring:
L92:       http:
L93:         clients:
L94:           reactive:
L95:             connector: jetty`
L96: 
L97:  | You can also use 【18†global configuration properties】 which apply to all HTTP clients.
L98: For more complex customizations, you can use 【19†`ClientHttpConnectorBuilderCustomizer`】 or declare your own 【20†`ClientHttpConnectorBuilder`】 bean which will cause auto-configuration to back off. This can be useful when you need to customize some of the internals of the underlying HTTP library.
L99: 
L100: For example, the following will use a JDK client configured with a specific 【21†`ProxySelector`†docs.oracle.com】:
L101: 
L102:   * Java
L103:   * Kotlin
L104: 
L105:     `import java.net.ProxySelector;
L106: 
L107:     import org.springframework.boot.http.client.reactive.ClientHttpConnectorBuilder;
L108:     import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
L109:     import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
L110: 
L111:     @Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
L112:     public class MyConnectorHttpConfiguration {
L113: 
L114:     	@Bean
L115:     	ClientHttpConnectorBuilder<?> clientHttpConnectorBuilder(ProxySelector proxySelector) {
L116:     		return ClientHttpConnectorBuilder.jdk().withHttpClientCustomizer((builder) -> builder.proxy(proxySelector));
L117:     	}
L118: 
L119:     }`
L120: 
L121:     `import org.springframework.boot.http.client.reactive.ClientHttpConnectorBuilder
L122:     import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean
L123:     import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration
L124:     import java.net.ProxySelector
L125: 
L126:     @Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
L127:     class MyConnectorHttpConfiguration {
L128: 
L129:     	@Bean
L130:     	fun clientHttpConnectorBuilder(proxySelector: ProxySelector): ClientHttpConnectorBuilder<*> {
L131:     		return ClientHttpConnectorBuilder.jdk().withHttpClientCustomizer { builder -> builder.proxy(proxySelector) }
L132:     	}
L133: 
L134:     }`
L135: ### WebClient Customization
L136: 
L137: There are three main approaches to 【6†`WebClient`】 customization, depending on how broadly you want the customizations to apply.
L138: To make the scope of any customizations as narrow as possible, inject the auto-configured 【11†`WebClient.Builder`】 and then call its methods as required. 【11†`WebClient.Builder`】 instances are stateful: Any change on the builder is reflected in all clients subsequently created with it. If you want to create several clients with the same builder, you can also consider cloning the builder with `WebClient.Builder other = builder.clone();`.
L139: To make an application-wide, additive customization to all 【11†`WebClient.Builder`】 instances, you can declare 【22†`WebClientCustomizer`】 beans and change the 【11†`WebClient.Builder`】 locally at the point of injection.
L140: 
L141: Finally, you can fall back to the original API and use `WebClient.create()`. In that case, no auto-configuration or 【22†`WebClientCustomizer`】 is applied.
L142: ### WebClient SSL Support
L143: 
L144: If you need custom SSL configuration on the 【13†`ClientHttpConnector`】 used by the 【6†`WebClient`】, you can inject a 【23†`WebClientSsl`】 instance that can be used with the builder’s `apply` method.
L145: 
L146: The 【23†`WebClientSsl`】 interface provides access to any 【24†SSL bundles】 that you have defined in your `application.properties` or `application.yaml` file.
L147: 
L148: The following code shows a typical example:
L149: 
L150:   * Java
L151:   * Kotlin
L152: 
L153:     `import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;
L154: 
L155:     import org.springframework.boot.webclient.autoconfigure.WebClientSsl;
L156:     import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
L157:     import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
L158: 
L159:     @Service
L160:     public class MyService {
L161: 
L162:     	private final WebClient webClient;
L163: 
L164:     	public MyService(WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder, WebClientSsl ssl) {
L165:     		this.webClient = webClientBuilder.baseUrl("https://example.org").apply(ssl.fromBundle("mybundle")).build();
L166:     	}
L167: 
L168:     	public Mono<Details> someRestCall(String name) {
L169:     		return this.webClient.get().uri("/{name}/details", name).retrieve().bodyToMono(Details.class);
L170:     	}
L171: 
L172:     }`
L173: 
L174:     `import org.springframework.boot.webclient.autoconfigure.WebClientSsl
L175:     import org.springframework.stereotype.Service
L176:     import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient
L177:     import reactor.core.publisher.Mono
L178: 
L179:     @Service
L180:     class MyService(webClientBuilder: WebClient.Builder, ssl: WebClientSsl) {
L181: 
L182:     	private val webClient: WebClient
L183: 
L184:     	init {
L185:     		webClient = webClientBuilder.baseUrl("https://example.org")
L186:     				.apply(ssl.fromBundle("mybundle")).build()
L187:     	}
L188: 
"}

