L75: Requires: Docker Compose 【49†2.20.3†github.com】 and later
L76: 
L77: With `include`, you can incorporate a separate `compose.yaml` file directly in your current `compose.yaml` file. This makes it easy to modularize complex applications into sub-Compose files, which in turn enables application configurations to be made simpler and more explicit.
L78: The 【50†`include` top-level element】 helps to reflect the engineering team responsible for the code directly in the config file's organization. It also solves the relative path problem that 【31†`extends`】 and 【30†merge】 present.
L79: 
L80: Each path listed in the `include` section loads as an individual Compose application model, with its own project directory, in order to resolve relative paths.
L81: 
L82: Once the included Compose application loads, all resources are copied into the current Compose application model.
L83: > Note
L84: >
L85: > `include` applies recursively so an included Compose file which declares its own `include` section, causes those files to also be included.
L86: ## 【51†Example】
L87: 
L88:     `include:
L89:       - my-compose-include.yaml  #with serviceB declared
L90:     services:
L91:       serviceA:
L92:         build: .
L93:         depends_on:
L94:           - serviceB #use serviceB directly as if it was declared in this Compose file`
L95: `my-compose-include.yaml` manages `serviceB` which details some replicas, web UI to inspect data, isolated networks, volumes for data persistence, etc. The application relying on `serviceB` doesn’t need to know about the infrastructure details, and consumes the Compose file as a building block it can rely on.
